n.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">9.3</span> Tokens &amp; Context</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">9.4</span> Fine-Tuning vs ICL</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">9.5</span> Safety &amp; Limitations</a></li></ul><span class="sidebar-section-label">Module 10: RAG Pipelines</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson1"><span class="sidebar-num">10.1</span> What is RAG?</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">10.2</span> Document Chunking</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">10.3</span> Embeddings &amp; Vectors</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">10.4</span> Building RAG</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">10.5</span> RAG Evaluation</a></li></ul></aside><button class="sidebar-toggle" aria-label="Toggle sidebar">☰</button><main class="main"><div class="container"><section class="hero"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="index.html">Associate</a><span class="sep">›</span><a href="#">Module 3</a><span class="sep">›</span><span>Data Loading and Inspection</span></div><span class="hero-eyebrow">Module 3 · Lesson 3</span><h1>Data Loading and Inspection <span class="accent">File Formats and Sources</span></h1><p class="hero-sub">Learn to load data from CSV, Excel, JSON, SQL databases, and web APIs. Master inspection techniques to understand structure, quality, and characteristics.</p></section><div class="objectives"><h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Master professional techniques for data loading and inspection</li><li>Apply industry best practices to data handling</li><li>Work with real-world data quality challenges efficiently</li><li>Implement reproducible and scalable solutions</li><li>Document and validate all data processing steps</li></ul></div><section class="section" id="sec1"><span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span><h2>Data Loading and Inspection</h2><p>Professional data science requires mastery of data loading and inspection as a foundational skill. This lesson covers essential techniques and real-world strategies used in production data pipelines.</p><h3>Key Principles</h3><p>Understanding the fundamental principles behind data loading and inspection enables you to handle edge cases and make informed decisions when facing new data challenges.</p><pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">pandas</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">pd</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">np</span>

<span class="cm"># Example approach for data loading and inspection</span>
<span class="cm"># This is a foundational pattern used across the industry</span></code></pre></section><section class="section" id="sec2"><span class="section-label">Practical Implementation</span><h2>Real-World Applications</h2><p>These techniques apply directly to production scenarios. Understanding when and how to use each approach is crucial for efficient data science work.</p><h3>Best Practices</h3><ul><li>Always validate input data before processing</li><li>Document all transformations and assumptions</li><li>Test edge cases and error conditions</li><li>Monitor performance on large datasets</li><li>Maintain version control for data processing scripts</li></ul></section><section class="section" id="sec3"><span class="section-label">Advanced Techniques</span><h2>Scaling to Production</h2><p>As datasets grow, efficient implementations become critical. Learn optimization strategies that maintain code clarity while improving performance.</p></section><section class="section" id="sec4"><span class="section-label">Common Pitfalls</span><h2>Avoiding Data Quality Issues</h2><p>Experienced data scientists recognize common pitfalls and have systematic approaches to prevent them. This section covers the most frequent mistakes and how to avoid them.</p></section><div class="exercise"><h4>Practice Exercises</h4><ol><li><strong>Hands-On Task 1:</strong> Complete a practical exercise applying the concepts from this lesson to a real dataset. Document your process and findings.</li><li><strong>Hands-On Task 2:</strong> Implement the techniques shown with your own data source. Validate results against expected outcomes.</li><li><strong>Challenge Exercise:</strong> Work with a complex, messy dataset that requires multiple techniques from this lesson. Demonstrate your understanding through thorough documentation.</li><li><strong>Performance Optimization:</strong> Optimize your solution for larger datasets. Measure performance improvements and document trade-offs.</li><li><strong>Production Readiness:</strong> Convert your solution into production-ready code with error handling, logging, and validation.</li></ol></div><div class="key-concept"><h4>Key Takeaways</h4><ul><li>Data Loading and Inspection is fundamental to professional data science work</li><li>Always prioritize data quality and validation in your workflows</li><li>Document decisions and maintain reproducibility throughout</li><li>Use appropriate tools and techniques for your specific data format and size</li><li>Test thoroughly before deploying to production systems</li><li>Performance optimization becomes crucial with large datasets</li><li>Industry best practices save time and prevent costly errors</li></ul></div><div class="nav-buttons"><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module3-lesson2" class="nav-btn prev">← Previous Lesson</a><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module3-lesson4" class="nav-btn next">Next Lesson →</a></div><section class="section" id="csv-advanced"><span class="section-label">Advanced CSV Techniques</span><h2>Handling Complex CSV Scenarios</h2><p>Real-world CSV files often have non-standard formatting, encoding issues, or large sizes. Learning to handle these scenarios efficiently separates professional data scientists from beginners.</p><h3>Large File Processing</h3><pre><code><span class="cm"># Process large files in chunks</span>
<span class="va">chunks</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">pd.read_csv</span>(<span class="st">'huge_file.csv'</span>, <span class="va">chunksize</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">10000</span>)
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">chunk</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">chunks</span>:
    <span class="fn">process</span>(<span class="va">chunk</span>)

<span class="cm"># Read only specific columns to save memory</span>
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">pd.read_csv</span>(<span class="st">'data.csv'</span>, <span class="va">usecols</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'Name'</span>, <span class="st">'Age'</span>, <span class="st">'Score'</span>])</code></pre><h3>Handling Encoding Issues</h3><p>International data often uses different character encodings. UTF-8 is standard, but you may encounter Latin-1, UTF-16, or others:</p><pre><code><span class="cm"># Try different encodings when UTF-8 fails</span>
<span class="va">encodings</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="st">'utf-8'</span>, <span class="st">'latin-1'</span>, <span class="st">'iso-8859-1'</span>, <span class="st">'cp1252'</span>]
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">enc</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">encodings</span>:
    <span class="kw">try</span>:
        <span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">pd.read_csv</span>(<span class="st">'data.csv'</span>, <span class="va">encoding</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">enc</span>)
        <span class="kw">break</span>
    <span class="kw">except</span>:
        <span class="kw">continue</span></code></pre><h3>Parsing Dates and Complex Types</h3><pre><code><span class="cm"># Automatically parse date columns</span>
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">pd.read_csv</span>(<span class="st">'data.csv'</span>, <span class="va">parse_dates</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'Date'</span>, <span class="st">'Timestamp'</span>])

<span class="cm"># Specify date format for faster parsing</span>
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">pd.read_csv</span>(<span class="st">'data.csv'</span>, 
              <span class="va">parse_dates</span><span class="op">=</span>{<span class="st">'Date'</span>: <span class="st">'%Y-%m-%d'</span>})

<span class="cm"># Handle multi-index CSV files</span>
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">pd.read_csv</span>(<span class="st">'data.csv'</span>, <span class="va">index_col</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>])</code></pre></section><section class="section" id="db-loading"><span class="section-label">Loading from Databases</span><h2>SQL and Data Warehouse Integration</h2><p>Professional data science often involves SQL databases, data warehouses, and APIs. Understanding how to connect and extract data is essential.</p><h3>SQL Database Connections</h3><pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">sqlite3</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">sqlalchemy</span>

<span class="cm"># SQLite (local databases)</span>
<span class="va">conn</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">sqlite3.connect</span>(<span class="st">'database.db'</span>)
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">pd.read_sql</span>(<span class="st">'SELECT * FROM table LIMIT 1000'</span>, <span class="va">conn</span>)

<span class="cm"># PostgreSQL with SQLAlchemy</span>
<span class="va">engine</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">sqlalchemy.create_engine</span>(
    <span class="st">'postgresql://user:password@localhost/dbname'</span>)
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">pd.read_sql</span>(<span class="st">'SELECT * FROM table'</span>, <span class="va">engine</span>)

<span class="cm"># MySQL connection</span>
<span class="va">engine</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">sqlalchemy.create_engine</span>(
    <span class="st">'mysql+pymysql://user:password@localhost/dbname'</span>)</code></pre></section><section class="section" id="data-profiling"><span class="section-label">Comprehensive Data Profiling</span><h2>Building Data Understanding Reports</h2><p>Systematic profiling reveals hidden patterns and quality issues. Professional data scientists create detailed profiling reports early in projects.</p><h3>Automated Profiling</h3><pre><code><span class="cm"># Comprehensive descriptive statistics</span>
<span class="va">stats</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">describe</span>(<span class="va">include</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'all'</span>)

<span class="cm"># Data quality metrics per column</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">col</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">columns</span>:
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">f</span><span class="st">"{col}:"</span>)
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">f</span><span class="st">"  Null %: {df[col].isnull().sum() / len(df) * 100:.2f}%"</span>)
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">f</span><span class="st">"  Unique: {df[col].nunique()}"</span>)
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">f</span><span class="st">"  Type: {df[col].dtype}"</span>)</code></pre></section><section class="section" id="api-data"><span class="section-label">Loading from APIs</span><h2>Web APIs and Real-time Data</h2><p>Many modern data sources provide API endpoints. Learning to work with REST APIs and JSON responses is increasingly important.</p><pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">requests</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">json</span>

<span class="cm"># Fetch data from REST API</span>
<span class="va">response</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">requests.get</span>(<span class="st">'https://api.example.com/data'</span>)
<span class="va">data</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">response.json</span>()
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">pd.DataFrame</span>(<span class="va">data</span>)

<span class="cm"># Handle pagination for large result sets</span>
<span class="va">all_data</span> <span class="op">=</span> []
<span class="va">page</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">1</span>
<span class="kw">while</span> <span class="kw">True</span>:
    <span class="va">r</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">requests.get</span>(<span class="st">'https://api.example.com/data'</span>, 
                    <span class="va">params</span><span class="op">=</span>{<span class="st">'page'</span>: <span class="va">page</span>})
    <span class="va">data</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">r</span>.<span class="fn">json</span>()
    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="kw">not</span> <span class="va">data</span>:
        <span class="kw">break</span>
    <span class="va">all_data</span>.<span class="fn">extend</span>(<span class="va">data</span>)
    <span class="va">page</span> <span class="op">+=</span> <span class="nm">1</span>
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">pd.DataFrame</span>(<span class="va">all_data</span>)</code></pre></section><section class="section" id="theory-deep"><span class="section-label">Theoretical Foundations</span><h2>Understanding the Mathematics and Science</h2><p>Professional mastery requires understanding underlying theory. While implementation frameworks change, fundamental principles endure. Invest time understanding the "why" behind each technique.</p><h3>Statistical Foundations</h3><p>Many data science techniques are rooted in statistics. Understanding distributions, hypothesis testing, and confidence intervals helps you use tools correctly and interpret results validly.</p><pre><code><span class="cm"># Example: Understanding sampling distributions</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">np</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="va">scipy</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">stats</span>

<span class="cm"># Generate sample and compute confidence interval</span>
<span class="va">data</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np.random.normal</span>(<span class="nm">100</span>, <span class="nm">15</span>, <span class="nm">1000</span>)
<span class="va">mean</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">data</span>.<span class="fn">mean</span>()
<span class="va">se</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">stats</span>.<span class="fn">sem</span>(<span class="va">data</span>)
<span class="va">ci</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">stats</span>.<span class="fn">t.interval</span>(<span class="nm">0.95</span>, <span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">data</span>)<span class="op">-</span><span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="va">loc</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">mean</span>, <span class="va">scale</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">se</span>)</code></pre><h3>Linear Algebra Essentials</h3><p>Matrix operations underlie many algorithms. Understanding vectors, matrices, and their operations helps you grasp high-dimensional concepts.</p></section><section class="section" id="case-studies"><span class="section-label">Real-World Case Studies</span><h2>Learning from Applied Examples</h2><p>Theory is essential, but application teaches the most. These case studies show how professionals solve real problems using the techniques covered in this lesson.</p><h3>Case Study 1: E-commerce Data</h3><p>An online retailer needs to understand customer behavior for targeted marketing. The analysis involves loading transaction data, cleaning it, and analyzing patterns.</p><p><strong>Data Sources:</strong> SQL database with transaction history, customer records, and product catalog. CSV files with marketing campaign responses.</p><p><strong>Challenges:</strong> Missing values in customer demographics, duplicates from multiple data sources, mixed data types requiring careful type conversion.</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong> Built a data pipeline that loads from multiple sources, deduplicates intelligently using customer ID and transaction time, fills missing demographics using group-based means, and validates final dataset against business rules.</p><h3>Case Study 2: Healthcare Analytics</h3><p>A hospital system analyzes patient outcomes to improve care quality. The challenge involves handling sensitive data, missing records, and complex relationships between tables.</p><p><strong>Lessons Learned:</strong> Healthcare data requires special care around privacy. Missing data often has meaning (certain populations miss appointments more). Data quality directly impacts patient safety.</p></section><section class="section" id="troubleshooting"><span class="section-label">Troubleshooting and Debugging</span><h2>When Things Go Wrong</h2><p>Even experienced data scientists encounter unexpected issues. Systematic debugging approaches help you solve problems efficiently.</p><h3>Common Issues and Solutions</h3><table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse;"><tr style="border: 1px solid #ddd;"><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;"><strong>Issue</strong></td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;"><strong>Diagnosis</strong></td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;"><strong>Solution</strong></td></tr><tr style="border: 1px solid #ddd;"><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Memory errors on large files</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">File size exceeds available RAM</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Use chunking, select specific columns, reduce dtype precision</td></tr><tr style="border: 1px solid #ddd;"><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Data type mismatches</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">String read as object instead of numeric</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Specify dtype during load or convert after loading</td></tr><tr style="border: 1px solid #ddd;"><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Unexpected null values</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Non-standard missing value indicators</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Check raw data, use na_values parameter</td></tr></table><h3>Debugging Workflow</h3><ol><li>Reproduce the issue with minimal example</li><li>Check assumptions about data format and structure</li><li>Use head(), info(), describe() to understand state</li><li>Test hypotheses with small subset</li><li>Document the issue and solution for future reference</li></ol></section><section class="section" id="performance-metrics"><span class="section-label">Performance Metrics and Optimization</span><h2>Measuring and Improving Efficiency</h2><p>Understanding performance characteristics helps you build systems that scale. Different approaches have different trade-offs in speed, memory, and complexity.</p><h3>Benchmarking Your Code</h3><pre><code><span class="cm"># Measure execution time for different approaches</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">timeit</span>

<span class="va">approach1</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"""</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> []
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">row</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">iterrows</span>():
    <span class="va">result</span>.<span class="fn">append</span>(<span class="va">row</span>[<span class="nm">1</span>][<span class="st">'value'</span>] <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">2</span>)
<span class="st">"""</span>

<span class="va">approach2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"""</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'value'</span>] <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">2</span>
<span class="st">"""</span>

<span class="fn">timeit</span>.<span class="fn">timeit</span>(<span class="va">approach1</span>, <span class="va">number</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">100</span>)
<span class="fn">timeit</span>.<span class="fn">timeit</span>(<span class="va">approach2</span>, <span class="va">number</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">100</span>)</code></pre><h3>Memory Optimization</h3><pre><code><span class="cm"># Profile memory usage</span>
<span class="va">df</span>.<span class="fn">memory_usage</span>(<span class="va">deep</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">True</span>).<span class="fn">sum</span>() <span class="op">/</span> <span class="nm">1024</span><span class="op">**</span><span class="nm">2</span>

<span class="cm"># Reduce memory by using appropriate dtypes</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'category_col'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'category_col'</span>].<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="st">'category'</span>)
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'big_int'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'big_int'</span>].<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="st">'int32'</span>)</code></pre></section><section class="section" id="team-collaboration"><span class="section-label">Collaborating on Data Projects</span><h2>Working in Teams Effectively</h2><p>Most real projects involve teams. Developing strong collaboration practices multiplies your impact.</p><h3>Code Review Best Practices</h3><ul><li>Keep changes focused and reviewable</li><li>Write clear commit messages explaining "why" not "what"</li><li>Respond constructively to feedback</li><li>Review others' code carefully and thoughtfully</li></ul><h3>Documentation Standards</h3><ul><li>README files explaining project purpose and usage</li><li>Docstrings for all functions and classes</li><li>Comments for non-obvious logic</li><li>Data dictionaries explaining all columns and transformations</li><li>Change logs documenting modifications over time</li></ul></section></div></main><footer><div class="container"><div class="footer-logo"><img src="../../assets/logo_only.png" alt="SustainSys" class="footer-logo-img"><div class="footer-logo-text">Sustain<span>Sys</span> AI Academy</div></div><p>© 2026 SustainSys. All rights reserved.</p><p><a href="https://sustainsys.co.uk">sustainsys.co.uk</a></p></div></footer><script src="../assets/course-nav.js"><\/script></body></html>